  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nCentral idea: This approach employs a multi-resolution optimization strategy combined with directional gradient descent to minimize the maximum overlap integral. Unlike traditional methods that use local search, it leverages directional gradients and resolution expansion to systematically escape local optima. The method treats the overlap integral as a piecewise maximum function that can be smoothed using exponential transformations to enable gradient-based optimization. Key improvements include gradient-based updates on directional rotations instead of point-updates, adaptive resolution control based on objective improvement, and a multi-start optimization strategy that evaluates candidates at multiple resolutions before final polishing.\n\nBottleneck addressed: Traditional stochastic local search methods often get trapped in local optima, particularly when the solution space contains multiple plateaus. This approach systematically explores higher resolutions while maintaining gradient guidance, whereas previous strategies depended too heavily on finely parameterized global optimizers without clear directionality.\n\nLimitations: Requires careful selection of smoothness parameters; may still fail for extremely constrained problems; gradient calculations are approximate due to smoothing, potentially affecting convergence speed.\n\nRationale: By using directional gradients combined with resolution expansion, we can both maintain the global search ability of earlier coarse methods while bringing the refinement capability of local methods. This balance is critical for very challenging optimization problems like the minimum overlap problem where energy landscape contains deep basins.\n\n<mathematical-specification>\nRepresentation: Piecewise constant step function with $n$ points over $[0,2]$\nVariables: $h = (h_1, ..., h_n) \\in [0,1]^n$ with $\\sum h_i = n/2$\nExact objective: $$C_5 = \\max_k \\frac{2}{n} \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h_i (1-h_{i+k})$$ over $k = -(n-1)$ to $n-1$\n\nConstraints: $\\forall i: 0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$, $\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h_i = n/2$\n\nTo enforce constraints, a logarithmic barrier is used: $- \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \\log(|h_i - 0^+|) - \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \\log(|1 - h_i - 0^+|)$ and $(\\sum h_i - n/2)^2$\n\nThe objective is smoothed by: $H(h) = \\tanh(K \\cdot T) \\cdot C(h) + (1 - \\tanh(K \\cdot T)) \\cdot (\\sum h_i - n/2)$\n\n<implementation-procedure>\n1. Initialization & parent validation:\n   - For seed=42, use initial_h_values directly if available (n=640)\n   - Else, artificially construct initial solution using:\n     ```\n     n0 = 64\n     h0 = np.zeros(n0)\n     h0[np.arange(0, n0, n0/(n0//2))]\n   ```\n   - Project to [0,1] using:\n     ```\n     h_clip = np.clip(h0, 0.05, 0.95)\n     normalize = h_clip.sum()\n     h_final = h_clip * (n/2) / normalize\n     ```\n\n2. Core update loop:\n   For each resolution n (initially n0=256):\n     a. Smooth the objective to: \n        $C_\u03b2(h) = -\\frac{1}{\\beta} \\log(\\langle \\exp(\\beta (1 - h_i(1 - h_{i+k})) \\rangle) +$ project penalty.\n     b. Compute first and second derivatives approximately via central finite differences\n     c. Perform directed gradient descent with:\n        - Adam optimizer (adaptive learning rate)\n        - Temperature annealing schedule\n        - Momentum factor\n     d. Evaluate post-optimization candidate\n\n3. Candidate acceptance:\n   - Track best solution at each resolution separately\n   - Only accept candidates maintaining gradient descent direction during smoother\n   - Fallback to previous recorded best if optimization instability detected\n\n4. Resolution transfer from parent:\n   ```\n   if n/2 in parent_solutions and abs(2/np.log2(n) - parent_interval) < 0.1:\n       h_new = interp_to(parent_solutions[n/2], parent_old_n, n)\n   else:\n       h_new = np.random.rand(n) * 0.8 + 0.1\n   ```\n   Scale and project as needed\n\n<search-choices>\n\n1. Default parameters:\n   - Smoothness parameter \u03b2  \n   - Learning rate: 0.1 * (1/2)^(iterations)\n   - Polynomial damping: 0.4\n\n2. Early stopping rules:\n   - 20 consecutive evaluations without improvement\n   - Seeking stagnation: if objective stops improving for 5 resolutions\n   - Escalation factors: use higher \u03b2 if underfitting detected\n\n3. Prioritized search path:\n   Multiple implementations are available for flexible execution:\n\n   <prioritized-search>\n   Strategy A (default): Use warm restarts between coordinates\n   Strategy B: Use penalty method for constraints\n   Strategy C: Use geometric progression of resolution\n\n   Choose strategy based on initialization success:\n\n   ```\n   if not initial_improvement:\n       algorithm_dtype = 'penalty_geometric'\n   else:\n       algorithm_dtype = 'restart_warm'\n\n   if np.min(h_initial) < 0.05 or np.max(h_initial) > 0.95:\n       choose_optimizer('adam')   # More robust for outliers\n   else:\n       choose_optimizer('adam_scheduled')  # More predictive\n   ```\n\n<compute-budget>\n\n1. Time estimation:\n   - Base resolution (256): ~15 seconds\n   - Next (512): ~60 seconds\n   - Peak resolving power (1280): ~3 + k*log(n) seconds\n\n2. Proposed schedule:\n   ```\n   if budget_remaining > 300:\n        resolution_multiplier = 2\n   elif budget_remaining > 150:\n        resolution_multiplier = 4\n   else:\n        resolution_multiplier = 8\n\n   n_next = n * resolution_multiplier\n   ```\n\n   Stop if next resolution exceeds available budget.\n\n<validation-and-contract>\n\n1. Post-optimization fitness evaluation:\n   - Verify 0<=h[i]<=1 for all i with tolerance 1e-6\n   - Validate sum(h) = n/2 with tolerance 1e-6\n   - Scoring: return (h, C_5, n)\n\n2. Fallback mechanism:\n   If new_c5 is invalid or shows sign of improvement only due to numerical issues:\n   ```\n   h_fallback = project_feasible(h, n)\n   # Force projected values to maintain energy balance\n   imbalance = np.abs(np.sum(h_fallback) - n/2)\n   if imbalance > 1e-3:\n        h_fallback = h_constrained(fallback_solution)\n   ```\n\n3. Scoring contract: \n   - Final evaluation must be performed using actual Erd\u0151s evaluator\n   - Return metric only if candidate passes all validation checks\n\n</validation-and-contract>",
